WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.110 --> 00:00:04.070
Good morning, Hank, it's Friday, the beginning
of the 9th annual Project for Awesome. You

00:00:04.070 --> 00:00:07.260
can head over to projectforawesome.com to
find a link to the 48 hour livestream, info

00:00:07.260 --> 00:00:10.990
on donating, and all the amazing perks, and
of course, to watch and vote for Project for

00:00:10.990 --> 00:00:15.009
Awesome videos. Your votes will determine
where much of the money raised is donated.

00:00:15.009 --> 00:00:18.100
But for the first half of the P4A, we're raising
money for Save the Children and the United

00:00:18.100 --> 00:00:20.280
Nations High Commission on Refugees.

00:00:20.280 --> 00:00:24.430
So you're probably familiar with the Syrian
refugee crisis. Millions of people have been

00:00:24.430 --> 00:00:28.840
forced to flee Syria as a result of a horrifying
civil war there, and both Save the Children

00:00:28.840 --> 00:00:33.620
and the UNHCR are providing massive support
to refugee camps from Turkey to Jordan to

00:00:33.620 --> 00:00:37.410
Lebanon, where more than a quarter of the
people currently living in the country are

00:00:37.410 --> 00:00:40.910
refugees, but these organizations both work
around the world and today, I wanna focus

00:00:40.910 --> 00:00:42.060
on Ethiopia.

00:00:42.060 --> 00:00:47.160
So since 2013, conflict in South Sudan has
forced more than 250,000 South Sudanese people

00:00:47.160 --> 00:00:51.550
to flee to the Gambella region of Ethiopia.
Most have left with only whatever belongings

00:00:51.550 --> 00:00:56.510
they can carry, and often ferry across the
river board in canoes. This woman, Sarah,

00:00:56.510 --> 00:01:01.150
explained to a Save the Children employee
succinctly why she and her family had to leave:

00:01:01.150 --> 00:01:05.330
"The government forces came and started killing
people, so we ran away."

00:01:05.330 --> 00:01:08.710
Once they get to Ethiopia, most of these people
make their way to a camp, where they then

00:01:08.710 --> 00:01:14.571
wait to register as refugees. More than 60%
of the refugees arriving in Gambella are children,

00:01:14.571 --> 00:01:18.680
and many meet this man, Johannus, a child
protection officer from Save the Children:

00:01:18.680 --> 00:01:24.390
"And here, they are many unaccompanied minors
who are separated from their parents."

00:01:24.390 --> 00:01:28.620
So Johannus works to help these unaccompanied
minors including these boys, all of whom arrived

00:01:28.620 --> 00:01:32.540
in Ethiopia separated from their families.
It's also Johannus's job to make sure that

00:01:32.540 --> 00:01:37.090
safe spaces are created and maintained for
children who live in the camps. Within the

00:01:37.090 --> 00:01:40.540
camps, Save the Children works to provide
basic medical care, including measuring physical

00:01:40.540 --> 00:01:44.869
progress to hopefully limit the stunting that
can lead to long-term physical and intellectual

00:01:44.869 --> 00:01:48.510
disabilities, and also to provide kids with
opportunities for play and learning.

00:01:48.510 --> 00:01:53.200
Meanwhile, the UNHCR works to get food and
basic shelter to the refugees which is a a

00:01:53.200 --> 00:01:57.930
massive task for a perennially underfunded
organization. For instance, this mother, Mary,

00:01:57.930 --> 00:02:01.750
explained that there's no firewood to cook
with in the camps, and no machines to mill

00:02:01.750 --> 00:02:06.530
grain. She said she wants to return to South
Sudan where her family was relatively prosperous

00:02:06.530 --> 00:02:08.920
before the fighting broke out, but it's too
dangerous.

00:02:08.920 --> 00:02:13.080
I know it's easy to feel hopeless in the face
of these complex challenges or to turn away

00:02:13.080 --> 00:02:18.610
from them. The global refugee crisis is overwhelming,
and it's easy to say that this is not our

00:02:18.610 --> 00:02:22.950
problem, that these boys are not our boys,
but in the past few years, we've seen what

00:02:22.950 --> 00:02:27.560
human history has shown us again and again,
that on this planet, there is no such thing

00:02:27.560 --> 00:02:32.220
as "other people's problems". Disease and
poverty and violence are shaped by political

00:02:32.220 --> 00:02:36.930
borders but they cannot be controlled by them.
Whether it's Ebola or antibiotic resistance,

00:02:36.930 --> 00:02:42.370
or the civil war in Syria, or the refugees
in Gamballa, we all lose when we imagine that

00:02:42.370 --> 00:02:47.230
there are no consequences to turning away
from the biggest human challenges. These unaccompanied

00:02:47.230 --> 00:02:52.180
children face an exceptionally difficult future,
but it is not a hopeless one. We know it isn't

00:02:52.180 --> 00:02:56.739
hopeless because we've seen the success that
humans have when we come together to support

00:02:56.739 --> 00:03:00.210
and empower the poorest and most disenfranchised
among us.

00:03:00.210 --> 00:03:04.560
Infant mortality has gone down by half in
the last 25 years. The percentage of kids

00:03:04.560 --> 00:03:09.540
in school has skyrocketed, and overall global
poverty has diminished, but the global refugee

00:03:09.540 --> 00:03:14.599
population hasn't been this high since World
War II, and more than half of them are children.

00:03:14.599 --> 00:03:18.880
These refugees, in accordance with international
law and also with whatever higher law might

00:03:18.880 --> 00:03:23.370
exist, need to be protected. We can't afford
to have a generation of marginalized kids

00:03:23.370 --> 00:03:27.959
with no access to healthcare or education,
and that's why I don't see donations to organizations

00:03:27.959 --> 00:03:33.330
like Save the Children or the UNHCR as gifts,
I see them as investments. We know that healthier,

00:03:33.330 --> 00:03:37.760
better educated kids become happier, more
productive adults, and we know that the cycle

00:03:37.760 --> 00:03:42.350
of poverty can be broken, but these kids need
our investment. So I hope you'll join me in

00:03:42.350 --> 00:03:46.120
supporting them, again, on this, the first
day of the Project for Awesome, all the donations

00:03:46.120 --> 00:03:49.400
to our charity The Foundation to Decrease
World Suck will be split between Save the

00:03:49.400 --> 00:03:53.290
Children and the UNHCR, and the money raised
after that will go to support charities chosen

00:03:53.290 --> 00:03:54.599
by you at projectforawesome.com.

00:03:54.599 --> 00:03:58.810
If you want to make a donation directly to
the Foundation, you can do that by clicking

00:03:58.810 --> 00:04:03.110
on this fancy new card that Google has made.
There's no fee with the transaction, which

00:04:03.110 --> 00:04:08.500
is amazing, and Google will match every dollar
donated through this card up to $100,000,

00:04:08.500 --> 00:04:12.120
so please help us take some of Google's money,
and again, you can go to projectforawesome.com

00:04:12.120 --> 00:04:16.480
to find the fundraiser there and all the perks
and stuff and to follow along with the livestream

00:04:16.480 --> 00:04:17.480
madness.

00:04:17.480 --> 00:04:20.999
Thank you, happy Project for Awesome, and
as we say in my hometown of Nerdfighteria,

00:04:20.999 --> 00:04:22.360
don't forget to be awesome.

00:04:22.360 --> 00:04:27.020
Endscreen, Hank always says I should make
an endscreen, yes, I know this video was more

00:04:27.020 --> 00:04:31.189
than 4 minutes long, and therefore, I will
probably be punished. I have accepted this

00:04:31.189 --> 00:04:34.690
fate. This is your last chance to use the
donation card unless you rewind the video.

00:04:34.690 --> 00:04:37.749
I will see you at projectforawesome.com and
in the livestream, bye!

